o, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-5t} & t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-5t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-5t} & t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-5t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-5t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369756 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e58bb6-6d90-5171-bba2-6ffa5a0c91d9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem Using a Given Matrix Exponential: Shifted Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6636fd0-4749-59a4-8b76-eef9487641a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(t-2)e^{3(t-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369758} {:answer/id #uuid "728c5e71-5fd1-5a98-9201-adf7dcefe959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(t-2)e^{3(t-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6636fd0-4749-59a4-8b76-eef9487641a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(t-2)e^{3(t-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369758} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2ff0f9d-dfdf-51f9-910c-18814c34489b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369757} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b6acca2-147b-5ca6-836a-a53266e20971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3(t-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b6acca2-147b-5ca6-836a-a53266e20971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3(t-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369761} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf476015-40d3-556b-9e37-8bcdbbec4844" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57c282b6-8400-51d9-92b2-8331bb08e0f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-335273" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(2) \\\\ y(2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n| $[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-2)}\\,x(2) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-2)} & (t-2){e}^{3(t-2)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-2)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,(t-2){e}^{3(t-2)}\\, \\\\ \\,{e}^{3(t-2)}\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e58bb6-6d90-5171-bba2-6ffa5a0c91d9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem Using a Given Matrix Exponential: Shifted Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03876557-bbe0-5e1c-b14e-d3042579aa8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5-t)e^{t-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03876557-bbe0-5e1c-b14e-d3042579aa8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5-t)e^{t-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369764} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad556fc7-cbfe-5afd-bca7-1d723006dc93" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369763} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcc2641f-a40e-5c96-a53e-7b50fd7d5061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e^{t-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcc2641f-a40e-5c96-a53e-7b50fd7d5061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e^{t-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369766} :answer-field/id #uuid "e15cc9a0-eab4-5c60-bdc7-1148bec9fcdc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "821b31cc-7595-5d0f-9fc7-1742bffe7ae8" :question/math-academy-id "q-335429" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(4) \\\\ y(4)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & t{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n| $[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-4)}\\,x(4) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t-4} & (t-4){e}^{t-4} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t-4}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,(5-t){e}^{t-4}\\, \\\\ \\,-{e}^{t-4}\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e58bb6-6d90-5171-bba2-6ffa5a0c91d9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem Using a Given Matrix Exponential: Shifted Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "977fb858-e00b-57cb-92fe-34346220f444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2(t+3)e^{7(t+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "977fb858-e00b-57cb-92fe-34346220f444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2(t+3)e^{7(t+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369769} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8ec36eb-3c0c-5793-8fc9-050c3a67e5b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369768} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef5fd92b-617a-562e-9fe8-338338f28723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e^{7(t+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef5fd92b-617a-562e-9fe8-338338f28723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e^{7(t+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369771} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f100bad-4b05-59d1-80d4-2cbc7cf84e38" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "77e4d91e-a2e3-5146-a546-2cdc835ec908" :question/math-academy-id "q-335430" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(-3) \\\\ y(-3)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{7t} & t{e}^{7t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n| $[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t+3)}\\,x(-3) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{7(t+3)} & (t+3){e}^{7(t+3)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{7(t+3)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,(-2t-6){e}^{7(t+3)}\\, \\\\ \\,-2{e}^{7(t+3)}\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e58bb6-6d90-5171-bba2-6ffa5a0c91d9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem Using a Given Matrix Exponential: Shifted Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92c3aadd-e7af-549f-8d5c-03c852b8d69e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369774} {:answer/id #uuid "34624695-7cec-5f24-8c6e-10a4a48ee719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369775} {:answer/id #uuid "52382715-a6d8-5856-a854-48678eaf8fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369776} {:answer/id #uuid "34d81aa0-6941-55bc-bfb1-b5f8c0db052b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369777} {:answer/id #uuid "deef3aa4-25d7-58b7-abab-46425c9deefd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34d81aa0-6941-55bc-bfb1-b5f8c0db052b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369777} :answer-field/id #uuid "286a01fc-1811-5be1-8e9f-2b9283454ef7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ab60b60-d1a6-5ef2-aaf8-6579001f22b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335448" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(1) \\\\ y(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1)) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e58bb6-6d90-5171-bba2-6ffa5a0c91d9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem Using a Given Matrix Exponential: Shifted Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c324707-3a02-5f25-9695-787ab1f2de99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t+2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t+2))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369781} {:answer/id #uuid "321c4cd9-1766-5a0a-aa57-0b4c7e7a8c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t+2)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t+2))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369782} {:answer/id #uuid "685df301-118e-5a58-bf75-9477e2eb66fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t+2)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t+2))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369783} {:answer/id #uuid "74687206-c506-56eb-beef-0d93774c24fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t+2)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t+2))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369784} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1a0761-a749-5ca4-ad21-ca6f377899cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t+2)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t+2))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd1a0761-a749-5ca4-ad21-ca6f377899cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t+2)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t+2))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369785} :answer-field/id #uuid "d57a8587-73bc-5db1-84f4-9abe5e0d13c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9955beb-b280-5cd1-9ad5-1f967a36652c" :question/math-academy-id "q-335451" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(-2) \\\\ y(-2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have \n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-(-2))}\\,x(-2) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t+2)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t+2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t+2)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t+2))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t+2)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t+2))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ff837c0-0ebb-5a9e-acb4-894261e0318c" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution: Homogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d22e282d-e968-57bd-a400-9b46374a3b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating both sides at $t=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369788} {:answer/id #uuid "af3238f3-8dad-51e6-a839-59c6b1b35639" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "applying the chain rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369789} {:answer/id #uuid "dcaece5d-98eb-5ff5-8dd0-8001e05689c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "using diagonalization of ${Φ}_{0}(t)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369790} {:answer/id #uuid "356ef949-654a-564c-8624-fc0a40a8c4da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating the equation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d22e282d-e968-57bd-a400-9b46374a3b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating both sides at $t=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369788} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c23d50a-e0c5-571b-9eb3-58edcba636a5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369787} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a224a950-6e6d-5bdb-89f8-51806ec8e0b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating both sides at $t=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369793} {:answer/id #uuid "f69a3de3-c983-54ae-bee2-c4c8ba371278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$ into $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369794} {:answer/id #uuid "b4291bd1-0f84-522a-b3fd-0b40c2f29760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "applying the chain rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369795} {:answer/id #uuid "c82b79b0-2020-523b-b78f-7b9a63fdbc80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating the equation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f69a3de3-c983-54ae-bee2-c4c8ba371278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$ into $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369794} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9ba404e-73f7-5d6b-81b1-2958204552dd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369792} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "557c4f89-4242-5f97-8005-e6446b47851a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369798} {:answer/id #uuid "fd813941-3036-583d-9df8-56a68146e586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369799} {:answer/id #uuid "bf8cc8cd-5d63-5bea-9bbc-226b8cb68088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369800} {:answer/id #uuid "8e37e049-d868-5f49-8ec5-9efe2cabc43f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd813941-3036-583d-9df8-56a68146e586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369799} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc25b498-2f29-5f2d-accc-61ff65ef3717" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b476f440-04ac-55c0-bbfe-8012f8f71f27" :question/math-academy-id "q-336477" :question/problem "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(0)=c$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,x(0)$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by  {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by substitution from lines {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Starting from $\\text{L2},$ we have the identity\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c.\n$$\n\nEvaluating this identity at $t=0$ gives\n\n$$\nx(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly line $\\text{L3}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{evaluating both sides at }t=0.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$, $\\text{L4},$ and $\\text{L5}.$ From $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c,\n$$\n\nand from $\\text{L4}$ we know ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.$ Substituting ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$ into $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c$ gives\n\n$$\nx(0)={I}_{n}c=c,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly line $\\text{L5}.$ Thus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{substituting }{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}\\text{ into }x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$, $\\text{L2},$ $\\text{L5},$ and $\\text{L6}.$ From $\\text{L1}$ we have ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At},$ and from $\\text{L5}$ we know $c=x(0).$ Substituting ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ from $\\text{L1}$ and $c=x(0)$ from $\\text{L5}$ into $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ (line $\\text{L2}$), we obtain\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,x(0).\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and substitution from $\\text{L1}\\text{ and }\\text{L5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229652 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ff837c0-0ebb-5a9e-acb4-894261e0318c" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution: Homogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d2ef95a-2f52-5509-8b54-aa93f3d318d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of matrix exponential" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228343} {:answer/id #uuid "1c92fcb2-7007-59ae-9bba-4af4976d7779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating both sides at $t = t_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228344} {:answer/id #uuid "57027a2d-9afa-5d36-ace7-2d96246c9bc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the properties of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57027a2d-9afa-5d36-ace7-2d96246c9bc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the properties of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228345} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0f7a63f-23ff-55e6-bdcc-76a81c7863f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228342} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fbea789-b450-5264-9cbf-abd3fc42ff3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "by substituting $t={t}_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369803} {:answer/id #uuid "4bce935a-ef9c-503f-865b-700800ca1757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "using the fact that ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369804} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a3743a-0336-55d5-b424-3b88edb16cbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "by substitution from line $\\text{L2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fbea789-b450-5264-9cbf-abd3fc42ff3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "by substituting $t={t}_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369803} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2216ce1-702a-57ab-b1d9-ab74958872fa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369802} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cec58bca-e058-5b38-8126-ad2515c389e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bt-B{t}_{0}=B(t-{t}_{0})$ for any $t$ and ${t}_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369807} {:answer/id #uuid "a93e4dab-4047-5b15-ac8e-c9060a1b01f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}$ when $X$ and $Y$ commute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369808} {:answer/id #uuid "4c787050-5597-56bf-a9dd-3e310cbf9f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${e}^{A{t}_{0}}={e}^{At}$ for any matrix $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369809} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b0afce-6fdd-5b29-8e3e-2c93a8e6856f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{Mt}{e}^{-M{t}_{0}}={e}^{M(t-{t}_{0})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a93e4dab-4047-5b15-ac8e-c9060a1b01f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}$ when $X$ and $Y$ commute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369808} :answer-field/id #uuid "da577183-f523-5e31-9052-33db386794ad" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da9645d1-ab1f-59b5-93f4-27c23f00e120" :question/math-academy-id "q-336639" :question/problem "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,c$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $x({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ is true by {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by the fact that {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is a fundamental matrix for the system, every solution of the system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c\\,\\text{for some constant vector }c.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L2}$ is true by $\\text{the properties of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Starting from $\\text{L3},$\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwe evaluate both sides at $t={t}_{0}$ to obtain\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L4}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ $\\text{by substituting }t={t}_{0}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L7}$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Since $At$ and $-A{t}_{0}$ commute, we can use the property \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into line $\\text{L7},$ we get line $\\text{L8}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by the fact that ${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}\\text{ when }X\\text{ and }Y\\text{ commute}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ff837c0-0ebb-5a9e-acb4-894261e0318c" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution: Homogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9756b0dd-50d5-50ec-800e-ae163a7c5e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "by substituting $t={t}_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369813} {:answer/id #uuid "92090a3f-e600-57db-b239-e88a332ffad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "using the fact that ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369814} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f841a8-4b9f-55fd-a4a4-049ed0ac219b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "by substitution from line $\\text{L2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9756b0dd-50d5-50ec-800e-ae163a7c5e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "by substituting $t={t}_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369813} :answer-field/id #uuid "475fcb80-1424-5462-a360-cae20cbfd378" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369812} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc0fa361-feb4-5a28-b378-30b69e163521" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{A{t}_{0}}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}=I{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369817} {:answer/id #uuid "d3733ef8-0386-5380-aa6f-923f1db46b56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369818} {:answer/id #uuid "2546ee97-a118-5674-9254-d2ac0fa15704" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}={e}^{A{t}_{0}}I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3733ef8-0386-5380-aa6f-923f1db46b56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369818} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa7f4509-2b5d-53c8-ad4d-5b60174cc47b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369816} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7448189-80c0-5cbe-983e-e3e36d61b5ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}$ when $X$ and $Y$ commute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369821} {:answer/id #uuid "62a444b8-bd0b-5620-aaca-bb2aa8358b68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369822} {:answer/id #uuid "36c9a3e7-719c-5080-ba8f-85cfe108f4c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${e}^{A{t}_{0}}={e}^{At}$ for any matrix $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7448189-80c0-5cbe-983e-e3e36d61b5ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}$ when $X$ and $Y$ commute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369821} :answer-field/id #uuid "11d9f67e-ce9e-571d-a3c7-274139a63ca9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c06019e-492b-58e8-b441-055a14820869" :question/math-academy-id "q-337109" :question/problem "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,c$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $x({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$  Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by the fact that {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by the fact that {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Starting from $\\text{L3},$\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwe evaluate both sides at $t={t}_{0}$ to obtain\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L4}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ $\\text{by substituting }t={t}_{0}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Line $\\text{L5}$ gives\n\n$$\n({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c.\n$$\n\nSince $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}={I}_{n}$, the right-hand side simplifies to $c$, so\n\n$$\nc=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nSimplify once, then apply $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}$, to obtain\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L6}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by the fact that $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L7}$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Since $At$ and $-A{t}_{0}$ commute, we can use the property \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into line $\\text{L7},$ we get line $\\text{L8}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by the fact that ${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}\\text{ when }X\\text{ and }Y\\text{ commute}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ff837c0-0ebb-5a9e-acb4-894261e0318c" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution: Homogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffe28982-653c-5939-835d-a2ccb30c9411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{A{t}_{0}}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}=I{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369826} {:answer/id #uuid "32600af2-86f3-5c68-b6cf-19eb20e20f69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369827} {:answer/id #uuid "73c194e4-916d-56d7-842a-bfffc2649a93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}={e}^{A{t}_{0}}I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32600af2-86f3-5c68-b6cf-19eb20e20f69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369827} :answer-field/id #uuid "31719cee-0dd9-5e45-813d-1bb46b0079d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369825} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a3b167a-3a1e-5a33-9ee4-ab2f9073af79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "left-multiplying $x(t)={e}^{At}c$ by ${e}^{-At}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369830} {:answer/id #uuid "33a97701-9497-5973-bc9a-5ee9d3160762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})$ into $x(t)={e}^{At}c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369831} {:answer/id #uuid "816df69c-d0c7-5f1c-93ff-722c59d6ebce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating $x(t)={e}^{At}c$ at $t=0$ and solving for $c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33a97701-9497-5973-bc9a-5ee9d3160762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})$ into $x(t)={e}^{At}c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369831} :answer-field/id #uuid "69e49f41-3cbb-58eb-b430-fb0279c56075" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369829} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6780975d-ce21-542b-897b-cfc796887506" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$At-A{t}_{0}=A(t-{t}_{0})$ for any $t$ and ${t}_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369834} {:answer/id #uuid "7275b08a-542e-593d-8443-bffbe10cdf92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${e}^{X+Y}={e}^{X}{e}^{Y}$ when $X$ and $Y$ commute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369835} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa7637f-c9a0-50ab-a0ec-e57dffa55bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}={e}^{A({t}_{0}-t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6780975d-ce21-542b-897b-cfc796887506" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$At-A{t}_{0}=A(t-{t}_{0})$ for any $t$ and ${t}_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369834} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f2a8e74-8c53-5a91-aff3-1d0ac19bea0e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb80bbdf-275a-5f01-a2dc-fafc9f40d1ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-337115" :question/problem "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,c$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $x({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by the fact that {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ can be obtained by {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L9}$ follows from $\\text{L8}$ by the fact that {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Line $\\text{L5}$ gives\n\n$$\n({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c.\n$$\n\nSince $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}={I}_{n}$, the right-hand side simplifies to $c$, so\n\n$$\nc=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nSimplify once, then apply $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}$, to obtain\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L6}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by the fact that $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L7}.$ Using line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,c.\n$$\n\nFrom line $\\text{L6}$, we know that $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}).$ Substituting this expression for $c$ into the formula for $x(t)$ gives\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L7}.$ Thus, line $\\text{L7}$ can be obtained by $\\text{substituting }c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\text{ into }x(t)={e}^{At}c.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L8}$ and $\\text{L9}.$ Starting from $\\text{L8},$\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwe rewrite the exponent using the algebraic identity $At-A{t}_{0}=A(t-{t}_{0})$ to get\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L9}.$ Thus, $\\text{L9}$ follows from $\\text{L8}$ by the fact that $At-A{t}_{0}=A(t-{t}_{0})\\text{ for any }t\\text{ and }{t}_{0}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ff837c0-0ebb-5a9e-acb4-894261e0318c" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution: Homogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c24a775-a2ea-5869-989e-08449ae4d3ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrating $x(t)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369839} {:answer/id #uuid "b866f10c-468e-5891-ab43-7f8357cc8b01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "using the identity $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369840} {:answer/id #uuid "e52d0878-989b-508d-a171-20e42a5f12ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplying both sides on the left by $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e52d0878-989b-508d-a171-20e42a5f12ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplying both sides on the left by $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369841} :answer-field/id #uuid "3eb5f316-9ea3-5d32-836d-27026f9f73b5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369838} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8580eeb0-31c1-5321-a470-1484bf5e35ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating $x(t)={e}^{At}c$ at $t={t}_{0}$ and solving for $c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369843} {:answer/id #uuid "4362c7dd-9819-5ee1-9b93-ffc687d79b84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "left-multiplying $x(t)={e}^{At}c$ by ${e}^{-At}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369844} {:answer/id #uuid "4e2ad203-123c-5a5c-af72-d2cf01a4a648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})$ into $x(t)={e}^{At}c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e2ad203-123c-5a5c-af72-d2cf01a4a648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})$ into $x(t)={e}^{At}c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369845} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b084338-c9a9-5a26-861a-23704e59f3c4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369842} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1c99914-a0b3-5e4e-9e29-b2606def1c06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the identity ${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{XY}$ for matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369847} {:answer/id #uuid "8333686f-b287-5b6c-99c1-b00bcb66fa6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the fact that ${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}$ when $X$ and $Y$ commute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369848} {:answer/id #uuid "06183b45-ac1c-5fce-8393-a758ef0b65a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the fact that $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8333686f-b287-5b6c-99c1-b00bcb66fa6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the fact that ${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}$ when $X$ and $Y$ commute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369848} :answer-field/id #uuid "574fb82f-0a72-58d8-aff3-dcff2c4ecee6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d9118b1-b0ff-5db4-a05c-ec3ad938a6f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-337121" :question/problem "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,c$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $x({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L4}$ by {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ can be obtained by {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L4}$ and $\\text{L5}.$ Starting from $\\text{L4},$\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwe multiply both sides on the left by $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}$ to get\n\n$$\n({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L5}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{multiplying both sides on the left by }({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L7}.$ Using line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,c.\n$$\n\nFrom line $\\text{L6}$, we know that $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}).$ Substituting this expression for $c$ into the formula for $x(t)$ gives\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L7}.$ Thus, line $\\text{L7}$ can be obtained by $\\text{substituting }c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\text{ into }x(t)={e}^{At}c.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L7}$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Since $At$ and $-A{t}_{0}$ commute, we can use the property \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into line $\\text{L7},$ we get line $\\text{L8}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by the fact that ${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}\\text{ when }X\\text{ and }Y\\text{ commute}.$"}]}
